// A hand-written entry point for a generated class. `@PROG@` is the name of
// that class; substitute it (the harness does, in test/stages/java.sh).
//
// THIS IS NOT GENERATED, AND THAT IS THE POINT. The extractor used to emit a
// `public static void main(String[])` into every program class, which made each
// output runnable and, for exactly that reason, not reusable: two extracted
// classes could not sit in one Java program, and the extractor was deciding
// something that is not its business. A lambda-box term is a VALUE. Whether to
// print it, hand it to another program, run it under a profiler, or evaluate it
// at all is the caller's decision -- so the caller writes these five lines.
//
// What a generated class gives you is `public static Object body()`: the
// program's value, computed on demand. Everything else here is a choice.
//
// The two choices this file makes, and why:
//
//   * `Rt.runMain` rather than calling `body()` directly. Lambda-box `fix`
//     compiles to ordinary, usually NON-TAIL Java recursion, so evaluation depth
//     is bounded by the thread stack -- and the stack of the primordial thread
//     that runs `main` is set by the OS (`ulimit -s`), not by the launcher's
//     `-Xss`, on several JVM/OS combinations. `runMain` runs the body on a
//     thread it creates with an explicitly requested stack size, which makes the
//     bound a property of the program instead of a property of how it was
//     launched. Tunable with `-Dlambox.stack=<bytes>`. See runtime/Rt.java.
//   * printing the result at all. `runMain` prints it and turns an exception
//     into a non-zero exit status, so a stack overflow cannot look like success
//     with no output. That is what makes the harness able to compare backends;
//     it is not something a library should do to you.
//
// The `Rt.Fn` wrapper is because `runMain` takes a function, so that it can
// evaluate the body ON the new thread rather than on this one. Its argument is
// ignored, and `Rt.BOX` is what gets passed.
//
// Compile all three together:
//   javac -d <dir> <dir>/Rt.java <dir>/@PROG@.java <dir>/Main.java
//   java -cp <dir> Main
public final class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Rt.runMain(new Rt.Fn() {
      public Object apply(Object ignored) {
        return @PROG@.body();
      }
    });
  }
}
